{
  "id": "Compound::DB00838 - Gene::2908",
  "verbalization": "Clocortolone, a fluorinated steroid, is implicated in the development of Dermatitis Perioral through a series of molecular interactions. The evidence suggests that Clocortolone causes Dermatitis Perioral, which is then mediated by different corticosteroids—Diflorasone, Prednicarbate, and Alclometasone—all of which bind to Nr3c1. These corticosteroids are part of the broader class of therapeutic glucocorticoids, which are known to modulate gene expression through the glucocorticoid receptor (Nr3c1). The shared ancestry of these compounds, particularly their classification as 21-hydroxy steroids or therapeutic glucocorticoids, indicates a common mechanism of action. The integration of these pathways highlights the role of Nr3c1 as a key receptor in the response to corticosteroids, with Clocortolone potentially acting through a similar mechanism to induce dermatological conditions. The evidence supports a direct link between Clocortolone and Nr3c1, mediated through corticosteroid-induced receptor activation, and underscores the importance of glucocorticoid signaling in the pathogenesis of dermatological disorders.",
